{
  "mechanism": {
    "summary": "A tokenizer turns text into a list of integers, one per token, and the model works only with those integers; on the way out the same tokenizer turns them back into text. Its vocabulary is learned by a merge loop: start from raw bytes, count neighbouring pairs across the corpus, add the most frequent pair as a new symbol, replace it everywhere, and repeat until the vocabulary reaches its target size. Encoding new text splits it into bytes, cuts it into chunks, then replays those merges in the order they were learned.",
    "sourceChunkIds": [
      "source-1#chunk-1",
      "source-1#chunk-2",
      "source-2#chunk-1"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Learning starts from raw bytes: the starting vocabulary is the 256 possible byte values, so any text at all can be written and nothing is ever unknown.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Count every pair of neighbouring symbols across the whole training corpus.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Take the most frequent pair, for example \"t\" followed by \"h\", and add it to the vocabulary as a new symbol, \"th\".",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Replace that pair everywhere in the corpus with the new symbol, count again, and repeat until the vocabulary reaches its target size.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "To encode new text, split it into bytes and pre-tokenize: letters stay together, punctuation splits off, a space sticks to the word after it, and digits are split individually.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-2#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Replay the learned merges in the order they were learned, joining every pair it can; merges never cross a pre-tokenization chunk border.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2",
          "source-2#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "A common word such as \"the\" collapses into a single token; an unusual word such as \"glimmerwick\" stays in several pieces because no merge ever joined them.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "The resulting token ids go to the model; on output, decoding maps the ids back to exactly the original text, including spaces, line breaks and emoji.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1",
          "source-2#chunk-1"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "plainSummary": "Text is chopped into pieces called tokens before the model sees it. The model only sees each piece as a number, never as letters. Common chunks are learned by joining the most frequent neighbouring pairs, again and again. Common words become a single token; rare words stay in several pieces. Encoding replays those joins, and decoding gives the original text back.",
  "analogies": [
    {
      "analogy": "A shorthand system for taking notes: first you invent a squiggle for the most common pair of letters, then squiggles for whole common words, so familiar phrases take almost no paper.",
      "matches": "The merge loop learns the most frequent pairs first and late merges build whole common words; encoding reuses those learned squiggles in the same order.",
      "breaksDown": "The real tokenizer works on bytes, not letters, and its byte starting set means no text is ever unrepresentable, while a shorthand writer can hit a name they have no squiggle for."
    },
    {
      "analogy": "Folding a sheet that has pre-marked creases: you can fold along a crease, but never across one.",
      "matches": "Pre-tokenization cuts text into chunks, and no merge is allowed to join pieces across a chunk border.",
      "breaksDown": "The borders come from rules about letters, punctuation, spaces and digits rather than physical creases, and a run of digits always stays as separate pieces."
    }
  ],
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "token",
      "plainMeaning": "A piece of text, from a single byte up to a whole word, that the tokenizer maps to one integer.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "tokenizer",
      "plainMeaning": "The program that turns text into token ids and turns ids back into text.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "vocabulary",
      "plainMeaning": "The fixed list of pieces the tokenizer can output; Saltmarsh's has 32,000 entries.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "merge list",
      "plainMeaning": "The ordered record of pair joins learned in training; encoding replays it from the start.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "byte-level",
      "plainMeaning": "Working on raw bytes, so any text can be written and there is never an \"unknown\" token.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "pre-tokenization",
      "plainMeaning": "Splitting text into chunks before merges run; merges never cross a chunk border.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "special tokens",
      "plainMeaning": "Reserved ids for document start, document end and padding; the application adds them, not ordinary text.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "embedding table",
      "plainMeaning": "The model's table with one row per vocabulary entry, so each entry has a storage cost.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "context window",
      "plainMeaning": "The limited amount of text, measured in tokens, that a model can hold at once.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "round trip",
      "plainMeaning": "Decoding the ids of an encoded text gives back exactly the original text.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "fact-1",
      "statement": "A language model never sees letters: a tokenizer turns text into a list of integers, one per token, and the model works only with those integers.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-2",
      "statement": "On the way out, the same tokenizer turns the integers the model produces back into text.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "background"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-3",
      "statement": "The vocabulary, the fixed list of pieces the tokenizer can output, has 32,000 entries in Saltmarsh.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-4",
      "statement": "Cutting text into single characters keeps the vocabulary tiny, but a paragraph becomes thousands of steps long and the model must spend its fixed context on spelling.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-5",
      "statement": "Cutting text into whole words keeps sequences short, but the vocabulary explodes: every name, typo and new word needs its own entry, and a word never seen in training has no integer at all.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-6",
      "statement": "Subword tokenizers sit between characters and words: frequent words get a single token of their own, while rare words are spelled out from smaller pieces the vocabulary does have.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-7",
      "statement": "Vocabulary learning starts from raw bytes, so the starting vocabulary is the 256 possible byte values and any text at all can be written, meaning nothing is ever unknown.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-8",
      "statement": "The merge loop counts every pair of neighbouring symbols in the corpus, adds the most frequent pair as a new symbol, replaces it everywhere, and repeats until the vocabulary reaches its target size.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-9",
      "statement": "The order of the merges is part of the tokenizer: early merges join very common pairs, late merges build whole common words and word endings.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-10",
      "statement": "Encoding splits new text into bytes and replays the learned merges in the order they were learned, joining every pair it can; the guide's example is a common word collapsing to a single token and an unusual word staying in several pieces.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-11",
      "statement": "A larger vocabulary makes sequences shorter, but every entry needs its own row in the model's embedding table and rare entries are seen too seldom during training to be learned well.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-12",
      "statement": "A smaller vocabulary is cheaper to store but makes every sequence longer.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-13",
      "statement": "On Saltmarsh's English test set a token covers about 4 characters of text on average, and the guide warns that this ratio is a property of English prose, not of tokenizers.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-14",
      "statement": "glyphcut works on bytes, so decoding an encoded text gives back exactly the original, including spaces, line breaks and emoji, it never needs an \"unknown\" token, and the same text with the same vocabulary always gives the same ids.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "background"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-15",
      "statement": "Before any merge runs, text is split into chunks that merges never cross; letters stay together, punctuation is split off, and a space is kept at the start of the word that follows it.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-16",
      "statement": "Digits are split individually, which keeps the vocabulary from spending entries on arbitrary numbers.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1",
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-17",
      "statement": "Some ids are reserved and never come from text: markers for document start, document end and padding, added by the application, so a user cannot type them.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "off"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-18",
      "statement": "You should count tokens, not words, to know whether a prompt fits a context window or what a request will cost, and only the tokenizer the model was trained with gives the true number.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-19",
      "statement": "A different vocabulary gives different ids: ids from one model's tokenizer mean nothing to another model.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "background"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-20",
      "statement": "Capital letters, leading spaces and rare spellings change how a word splits, so \"Paris\", \" Paris\" and \"PARIS\" can be one, one and three tokens.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-21",
      "statement": "At Corrowby every model request is billed by the token and every conversation must fit the model's context window, also measured in tokens; before counting tokens properly they estimated both from word counts.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-22",
      "statement": "For English support replies, a word came to about 1.3 tokens on average: short common words are a single token, while product names, long words and unusual capitals split into several.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-23",
      "statement": "A language rare in the tokenizer's training text needed about 3 times as many tokens to say the same thing, so the same answer cost more and used more of the context window.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-24",
      "statement": "In source code, runs of spaces used for indentation and identifiers written in mixed case split into many tokens, and numbers are split digit by digit, so an order number is as many tokens as it has digits.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-25",
      "statement": "Corrowby believes digit-by-digit splitting is why the model sometimes slips on long arithmetic: it never sees a number as a whole thing, only as a row of digits.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "low",
      "angle": "off"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-26",
      "statement": "After the change, Corrowby counts tokens with the exact tokenizer of the model it calls, writes prompt and reply budgets in tokens, and produces per-language cost reports.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high",
      "angle": "serves"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-27",
      "statement": "The often-quoted \"about four characters\" per token rule held for Corrowby's English text but not for code or for the rarer languages, and they have not agreed on a single rule to replace it.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "medium",
      "angle": "serves"
    }
  ],
  "numbers": [
